-- title:
--   Kernel of the formal coordinates is the [p^v]-series ideal
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and $\mathcal O$ a commutative ring in which $p$ is a non-zero-divisor, equipped with an $\mathcal O$-algebra structure on $\mathbb Z/p$ whose structure map has kernel the ideal $(p)$, and which is complete and separated for the $(p)$-adic topology. Fix $h_0$ and a family $R_0(v)$, $v \ge 0$, of commutative rings, each a cocommutative Hopf $\mathcal O$-algebra that is finite and free as an $\mathcal O$-module, with surjective bialgebra maps $t_0(v) \colon R_0(v+1) \to R_0(v)$ such that $\operatorname{finrank}_{\mathcal O} R_0(v) = p^{v h_0}$ and $\ker t_0(v)$ is the $p^v$-torsion ideal of $R_0(v+1)$, namely the image of the augmentation ideal (the kernel of the counit) under the $p^v$-th convolution power of the identity; assume each $R_0(v)$ is local. Fix $d$ and surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra maps $\pi_v \colon \mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]] \to R_0(v)$ with $t_0(v) \circ \pi_{v+1} = \pi_v$, with $\varepsilon(\pi_v(X_i)) = 0$ and $\pi_v(X_i)$ in the radical of $(p) \subseteq R_0(v)$, jointly injective (a power series killed by all $\pi_v$ is $0$) and jointly surjective (every $t_0$-compatible family $(z_v)$ is $(\pi_v(G))_v$ for some $G$). Let $F$ be a $d$-dimensional formal group law over $\mathcal O$ (a $d$-tuple of power series in $\mathrm{Fin}\,d \oplus \mathrm{Fin}\,d$ variables with vanishing constant term, identity linear coefficients on each summand, and associative), and assume for all $v, i$ that $\Delta(\pi_v(X_i))$ equals the $(p)$-adic evaluation of $F_i$ in $R_0(v) \otimes_{\mathcal O} R_0(v)$ at the point sending the left variables to $\pi_v(X_j) \otimes 1$ and the right variables to $1 \otimes \pi_v(X_j)$. Then for every $v$, $\ker \pi_v$ is the ideal generated by the $d$ components of the $p^v$-th iterate series of $F$, defined recursively by $0$ at step $0$ and by substituting the previous iterate into the left variables and $X$ into the right variables of $F$.
--
--   This is the second assertion of Tate's Proposition 1 on $p$-divisible groups: a connected $p$-divisible group over such an $\mathcal O$ is the $p^v$-torsion of its formal group law, so that the $v$-th level ring is $\mathcal O[[X_1,\dots,X_d]]/([p^v]_F)$. It is used in [`PDivisibleGroup.exists_mvFormalGroup_connectedComponent_tower_of_isLocalRing_cartierDual`](thm.html#PDivisibleGroup.exists_mvFormalGroup_connectedComponent_tower_of_isLocalRing_cartierDual), which produces the formal group law attached to the connected component of a $p$-divisible group together with this description of its levels.
